From: SacredTrees <121399723+SacredTrees@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 23:55:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] JSC m68k: take the task hooks off at exit

Patch 0033 puts a switch and a launch hook (tc_Switch, tc_Launch, with
TF_SWITCH and TF_LAUNCH) on every task that runs JavaScript, and never
took them off. A program started from a Shell runs on the Shell's own
process, which lives on after the program has gone and its code is freed.
Exec's task switcher went on calling the two hooks in that freed memory
each time the Shell's task was switched; when the next program was loaded
over it the machine hung (a jump into half-loaded code in supervisor mode
with interrupts off, then address 0).

jsc_m68k_detach_thread() takes the hooks and the two flags off the
thread that calls it, inside Disable(), and frees its slot. What the task
had before our hooks (kept when they go on) is put back, in case another
program had hooks of its own there; as a rule that is nothing. A
destructor calls it when the program ends, after every other destructor.

The program's other threads keep their hooks: they may still be in the
interpreter, and their tasks end with the program. (Taking them off every
thread at exit stalled one run in eight in the lab.)

Seen with the test viewer run twice in a row from one Shell, the first
run on a page with scripts (17 November 2026). The browser started from
a Shell that stays open had the same fault.

Team 2
---
  Source/JavaScriptCore/llint/LowLevelInterpreter.cpp | 39 +++++++++++++++++++++
  1 file changed, 39 insertions(+)

diff --git a/Source/JavaScriptCore/llint/LowLevelInterpreter.cpp b/Source/JavaScriptCore/llint/LowLevelInterpreter.cpp
--- a/Source/JavaScriptCore/llint/LowLevelInterpreter.cpp
+++ b/Source/JavaScriptCore/llint/LowLevelInterpreter.cpp
@@ -409,6 +409,10 @@
     struct Task* task;
     uint32_t registers[M68KRegisterWords];
     void* stackPointerField;
+    // What the task had before our hooks, to put back at exit.
+    decltype(Task::tc_Switch) switchBefore;
+    decltype(Task::tc_Launch) launchBefore;
+    uint8_t flagsBefore;
 };
 
 static M68KThreadRegisters m68kThreads[M68KThreadSlots];
@@ -494,11 +498,46 @@
         thread = m68kThreadFor(nullptr);
     RELEASE_ASSERT(thread);
     thread->task = task;
+    thread->switchBefore = task->tc_Switch;
+    thread->launchBefore = task->tc_Launch;
+    thread->flagsBefore = task->tc_Flags & (TF_SWITCH | TF_LAUNCH);
     task->tc_Switch = reinterpret_cast<decltype(task->tc_Switch)>(jsc_m68k_task_switch);
     task->tc_Launch = reinterpret_cast<decltype(task->tc_Launch)>(jsc_m68k_task_launch);
     task->tc_Flags |= TF_SWITCH | TF_LAUNCH;
     Enable();
 }
+
+// At exit the hooks come off the thread that ends the program, and what it
+// had before them goes back (nothing, as a rule). That thread is often not
+// ours to end: a program started from a Shell runs on the Shell's own
+// process, which lives on after the program's code is freed. Left on,
+// exec's task switcher went on calling the hooks in that freed memory, and
+// the next program loaded there hung the machine (17 November 2026: a
+// second run of the viewer straight after a first that had run a page's
+// scripts). The program's other threads keep theirs: they may still be in
+// the interpreter, and their tasks end with the program.
+extern "C" void jsc_m68k_detach_thread()
+{
+    struct Task* task = SysBase->ThisTask;
+    Disable();
+    M68KThreadRegisters* thread = m68kThreadFor(task);
+    if (thread) {
+        if (task->tc_Launch == reinterpret_cast<decltype(task->tc_Launch)>(jsc_m68k_task_launch)) {
+            task->tc_Flags = (task->tc_Flags & ~(TF_SWITCH | TF_LAUNCH)) | thread->flagsBefore;
+            task->tc_Switch = thread->switchBefore;
+            task->tc_Launch = thread->launchBefore;
+        }
+        thread->task = nullptr;
+    }
+    Enable();
+}
+
+// Priority 101: after every other destructor, which may still run scripts.
+__attribute__((destructor(101))) static void detachM68KThreadAtExit()
+{
+    jsc_m68k_detach_thread();
+}
+
 #endif
 
 JSValue CLoop::execute(OpcodeID entryOpcodeID, void* executableAddress, VM* vm, ProtoCallFrame* protoCallFrame, bool isInitializationPass)
